      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "IL-15 is a T(H)1-related cytokine that shares many biologic activities with IL-2. Both cytokines bind a specific alpha subunit, and they share the same beta and gamma common receptor subunits for signal transduction. IL-15 has recently been shown to be upregulated in T cell-mediated inflammatory disorders, such as rheumatoid arthritis and inflammatory bowel diseases. However, the role and expression of IL-15 in inflammatory lung disease has not been investigated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "In the present study we have evaluated the expression of IL-15 mRNA and protein in bronchial biopsy specimens obtained from patients with sarcoidosis (n = 8), tuberculosis (n = 7), chronic bronchitis (n = 10), and bronchial asthma (n = 8) and compared its expression with that seen in normal control subjects (n = 11)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In situ hybridization and immunocytochemistry were used to detect the number of cells expressing IL-15 mRNA and protein, respectively, within sections of bronchial tissues from all subject groups. In addition, double immunocytochemistry was used to characterize the cellular source of IL-15."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The number of IL-15(+) cells was significantly higher within tissue from patients with sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and chronic bronchitis compared with that in asthmatic patients and normal control subjects. Similar results were obtained for IL-15 immunoreactivity by using immunohistochemistry. Furthermore, double immunostaining revealed that neutrophils and macrophages are the major source of IL-15."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "These results suggest that the expression of IL-15 may be associated with T(H)1-mediated chronic inflammatory diseases of the lung."
        }
      ]},
